      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We are tasked with finding the smallest positive integer \\( b \\) such that the quadratic expression \\( x^2 + bx + 2008 \\) can be factored into a product of two binomials with integer coefficients. This means we can write:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + bx + 2008 = (x + p)(x + q)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are integers. Expanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n(x + p)(x + q) = x^2 + (p+q)x + pq\n\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients, we identify:\n\\[\nb = p + q\n\\]\n\\[\npq = 2008\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the pairs \\((p, q)\\) such that \\( pq = 2008 \\) and then determine the corresponding \\( b = p + q \\). We start by finding all pairs of integers \\((p, q)\\) whose product is 2008.\n\nThe prime factorization of 2008 is:\n\\[\n2008 = 2^3 \\times 251\n\\]\n\nThe number of pairs \\((p, q)\\) such that \\( pq = 2008 \\) is given by the factor pairs of 2008. The factor pairs are:\n\\[\n(1, 2008), (-1, -2008), (2, 1004), (-2, -1004), (4, 502), (-4, -502), (8, 251), (-8, -251)\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( b = p + q \\) for each pair:\n\n1. For \\((p, q) = (1, 2008)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 1 + 2008 = 2009\n   \\]\n\n2. For \\((p, q) = (-1, -2008)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = -1 + (-2008) = -2009\n   \\]\n\n3. For \\((p, q) = (2, 1004)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 2 + 1004 = 1006\n   \\]\n\n4. For \\((p, q) = (-2, -1004)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = -2 + (-1004) = -1006\n   \\]\n\n5. For \\((p, q) = (4, 502)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 4 + 502 = 506\n   \\]\n\n6. For \\((p, q) = (-4, -502)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = -4 + (-502) = -506\n   \\]\n\n7. For \\((p, q) = (8, 251)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 8 + 251 = 259\n   \\]\n\n8. For \\((p, q) = (-8, -251)\\):\n   \\[\n   b = -8 + (-251) = -259\n   \\]\n\nAmong all these values of \\( b \\), the smallest positive integer is \\( b = 259 \\).\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( b \\) for which \\( x^2 + bx + 2008 \\) factors into a product of two binomials is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{259}\n\\]",
